  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 134 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Large area of high pressure (1025 mb) centered \nover sern Ontario will remain our controlling weather feature \ntonight and through the daylight hours on Sunday. Daytime flat \ncumulus field this afternoon will give way to mostly clear and \nlight wind conditions tonight. While some high clouds move in \nlate across nrn NY, conditions once again appear favorable for \nlocally dense fog 06-12Z east of the Green Mountains in Vermont.\nGood radiational cooling conditions overall, with overnight \nlows mainly in the 40s, except low-mid 50s in the immediate \nChamplain Valley. PoPs NIL overnight. \n\nPBL winds switch light southerly Sunday as sfc anticyclone \nshifts to the Gulf of Maine. Channeled flow will yield somewhat \nstronger winds over Lake Champlain of 10-15 kt. This will allow \nfor slightly warmer afternoon highs in the upper 70s to lower \n80s. Humidity remains modest though, with 2-m dewpoints \ngenerally in the mid-upper 50s. \n\nModest 850-500mb height falls and weak sfc low/frontal system \nbring increasing clouds and widespread showers for Sunday night \ninto early Monday. Rainfall amounts generally 0.25-0.50\" with \nthis system...and generally beneficial. With that said, some of \nthe statistical guidance, including the probability matched mean\n(PMM) QPF from the REFS indicates potential for around 1\" \nrainfall from Jay Peak eastward across the Northeast Kingdom. \nStill not enough to cause any hydro/flooding concerns, but \nhighest rainfall totals appear likely in the NEK aided by modest\nlow-level frontogenesis forcing. Drier conditions return Monday\nnight into Tuesday with high pressure. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heading into the middle of next week, guidance \ncontinues to show broad cyclonic flow dominating the region, \nwith a frontal boundary pushing through sometime Wednesday night\ninto Thursday. This front will bring increased shower and \nthunderstorms, although there's still a little uncertainty \nregarding the details at this time range, especially with how \nlong showers linger across the area. After this system, a period\nof drier weather looks to return for the latter half of the \nweek. High temperatures for next week will gradually trend back \ntowards the 70s areawide after a few days in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s earlier in the week. Overall, a period of rather \nunimpactful and seasonable weather is expected for much of next \nweek.",
